---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Driftstack"
  description="Pixel-identical iPhone Safari sessions in the cloud. Bit-identical canvas, WebGL, audio, and user-agent — no runtime patching, no stealth plugins, nothing for detection to find. API, SDK, or GUI. Try it for $2.99."
>
  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <div class="absolute inset-0 grid-bg opacity-40" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="relative mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-16 sm:pb-28 sm:pt-24 md:pt-32">
      <div class="grid gap-10 sm:gap-12 md:grid-cols-2 md:items-center">
        <div class="animate-fade-up">
          <p class="section-label">iPhone Safari · on demand</p>
          <h1
            class="mt-4 text-[1.5rem] font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink-primary sm:mt-5 sm:text-4xl md:text-5xl lg:text-6xl"
            style="letter-spacing: -0.02em; line-height: 1.15;"
          >
            <span class="bg-gradient-to-br from-ink-primary via-ink-primary to-glow-red bg-clip-text text-transparent">
              Indistinguishable iPhone Safari.
            </span>
            <span class="block">Programmable from any language.</span>
          </h1>
          <p class="mt-4 max-w-prose text-sm text-ink-secondary leading-relaxed sm:mt-5 sm:text-base md:text-lg">
            Real WebKit — the engine every iPhone ships. Canvas + WebGL
            hashes match the millions of iPhones in the wild, not the
            unique-per-session leak every Chromium-stealth API surfaces.
            Drive sessions from TypeScript, Python, Go, or the GUI.
          </p>
          <div class="mt-5 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3 sm:mt-8 sm:gap-4">
            <a href="/pricing#trial-pack" class="btn-primary">Start for $2.99</a>
            <a href="/comparison" class="btn-secondary">Compare the alternatives</a>
          </div>
          <p class="mt-3 text-[11px] text-ink-muted sm:mt-4 sm:text-xs">
            16 hours of session time · 14-day window · one trial per account.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="code-preview relative">
          <div class="code-window-chrome">
            <span class="pip"></span>
            <span class="pip"></span>
            <span class="pip"></span>
            <span class="ml-3 truncate text-[11px] text-ink-muted sm:text-xs">driftstack-typescript-sdk</span>
            <button
              type="button"
              data-copy-target="hero-sdk-code"
              class="ml-auto rounded border border-white/10 px-2 py-0.5 text-[10px] font-mono text-ink-muted transition hover:border-glow-red hover:text-glow-red"
              aria-label="Copy code to clipboard"
            >
              <span data-copy-label>Copy</span>
            </button>
          </div>
          <pre class="max-h-[220px] overflow-x-auto p-3 text-[9.5px] leading-relaxed text-ink-primary sm:max-h-none sm:p-5 sm:text-xs md:text-sm"><code id="hero-sdk-code">{`import { Driftstack } from '@driftstack/sdk';

const ds = new Driftstack({ apiKey: process.env.DRIFTSTACK_API_KEY });

const session = await ds.sessions.create({
  archetype: 'iphone16pro_ios18_7_safari26_4',
  label: 'target-flow',
});

try {
  await ds.sessions.navigate(session.id, { url: 'https://target.example' });
  await ds.sessions.capture(session.id, { kind: 'screenshot' });
  const state = await ds.sessions.getState(session.id);
  console.log(state.url, state.title);
} finally {
  await ds.sessions.destroy(session.id);
}`}</code></pre>
          <!-- Horizontal-scroll affordance: fade on the right edge so
               readers know the code preview can be panned on mobile. -->
          <div class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-y-12 right-0 w-8 bg-gradient-to-l from-surface-base/80 to-transparent sm:hidden"></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- M.2 — "Two ways to drive it" section, refreshed per Plan Item 3.
       The hero above shows the imperative SDK as a real code block;
       this section now shows the AI-agent UX as a stylized visual
       mockup (chat transcript + LiveKit preview pane) so customers
       SEE the agent experience instead of reading another large
       <pre><code> block. Static HTML/CSS — no live LiveKit embed
       yet (a real auth-gated session embed lands as a follow-up
       once the Q.0 EG-API-1.6 propagation + AI-B1.b activation
       arrive). Mobile: md:grid-cols-[1fr_1fr] collapses to single
       column, chat on top + preview below; preview min-height
       stays 280px so the preview pane doesn't shrink to a sliver. -->
  <section class="relative border-t border-white/10">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-20">
      <p class="section-label">Two ways to drive it</p>
      <h2
        class="mt-5 max-w-3xl text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink-primary sm:mt-6 sm:text-3xl md:text-4xl"
        style="letter-spacing: -0.02em;"
      >
        Code it precisely, or just describe what you want.
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-prose text-sm text-ink-secondary leading-relaxed sm:mt-5 sm:text-base">
        Engineers drive sessions directly with the SDK (see the hero
        example). Everyone else can open the dashboard, type the task
        in plain English, and watch the agent execute it live — with
        the option to export the result as code anytime.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-10 grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-2 md:items-stretch">
        <!-- Chat-panel mockup. Renders as a faux conversation between
             the customer and the AI agent. Pure CSS; no JS needed. -->
        <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-white/10 bg-surface-raised">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-2 border-b border-white/10 bg-white/[0.02] px-5 py-3 text-xs text-ink-muted">
            <span class="size-2 rounded-full bg-glow-red"></span>
            <span class="font-mono">AI agent · iPhone 16 Pro</span>
          </div>
          <div class="space-y-4 p-5 text-sm">
            <div class="flex gap-3">
              <span
                class="flex size-7 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-glow-red/20 text-xs"
                >you</span
              >
              <p class="rounded-lg bg-white/[0.03] px-4 py-3 text-ink-primary">
                Log in with the test account, open the revenue
                dashboard, and screenshot the chart for the last 30 days.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-3">
              <span
                class="flex size-7 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-ink-primary/10 text-xs"
                >AI</span
              >
              <div class="space-y-2 text-ink-secondary">
                <p class="rounded-lg bg-white/[0.02] px-4 py-3">
                  Planning 4 steps:
                  <span class="font-mono text-glow-red">navigate</span>,
                  <span class="font-mono text-glow-red">fillForm</span>,
                  <span class="font-mono text-glow-red">click</span>,
                  <span class="font-mono text-glow-red">screenshot</span>.
                </p>
                <p class="rounded-lg bg-white/[0.02] px-4 py-3">
                  Logged in. Navigating to /dashboard/revenue.
                </p>
                <p class="rounded-lg bg-white/[0.02] px-4 py-3">
                  Screenshot captured ·
                  <span class="text-glow-red underline underline-offset-4">view</span>
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="flex items-center justify-between border-t border-white/10 bg-white/[0.02] px-5 py-3 text-xs">
            <span class="text-ink-muted">A few seconds · 4 steps planned</span>
            <span class="text-glow-red">Export as SDK code →</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Live-preview pane stub. Placeholder for a real
             LiveKit-streamed iPhone-frame mockup; the static
             monospace label avoids implying a live stream exists
             at this URL today. -->
        <div class="flex flex-col overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-white/10 bg-surface-raised">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-2 border-b border-white/10 bg-white/[0.02] px-5 py-3 text-xs text-ink-muted">
            <span class="size-2 rounded-full bg-green-500"></span>
            <span class="font-mono">Live preview — &lt;livekit-room&gt;</span>
          </div>
          <div class="relative flex min-h-[280px] flex-1 items-center justify-center bg-black/40">
            <span class="font-mono text-xs text-ink-muted">
              [ iPhone Safari · live preview ]
            </span>
          </div>
          <div class="border-t border-white/10 bg-white/[0.02] px-5 py-3 text-xs text-ink-muted">
            Watch in real time · 95ms median latency
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-6 max-w-prose text-sm text-ink-muted">
        The chat agent is the fast path for one-off tasks and
        explorations. Click
        <strong class="text-ink-primary">Export as SDK code</strong> to
        lift the decomposed intent log into a reusable script — same
        engine, same fingerprint, no agent overhead on repeat runs.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- F-2 / F-5 (Issue 4 + 5 + 6 + 7) — replaces the prior R7
       compliance-badges 4-up strip (EU-resident / Stripe-billed /
       GDPR DPA / Source escrow) with product-differentiator signals.
       Compliance copy lives on /trust + /legal pages and on the
       footer meta-link row; the homepage's splash slot below the
       hero is for differentiators a competitor can't claim. The
       prior aspirational-launch eyebrow was stripped per Issue 5
       (launch-only framing — no "Pre-launch" labelling). -->
  <section class="relative border-t border-white/10">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-10">
      <p class="text-center font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-ink-muted">
        What sets us apart
      </p>
      <div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-2 gap-x-8 gap-y-6 sm:grid-cols-4">
        <div class="flex flex-col items-center gap-1.5 text-center">
          <span class="text-base font-semibold text-ink-primary">Population-matched</span>
          <span class="text-xs text-ink-muted">Canvas + WebGL hashes match the real-iPhone population — not unique-per-session like every other API</span>
        </div>
        <div class="flex flex-col items-center gap-1.5 text-center">
          <span class="text-base font-semibold text-ink-primary">Zero detection surface</span>
          <span class="text-xs text-ink-muted">No runtime JS patching, no stealth bundle for fingerprinters to spot</span>
        </div>
        <div class="flex flex-col items-center gap-1.5 text-center">
          <span class="text-base font-semibold text-ink-primary">Apple's WebKit, not Chromium</span>
          <span class="text-xs text-ink-muted">Built from Apple's WebKit source — same engine your iOS users actually run</span>
        </div>
        <div class="flex flex-col items-center gap-1.5 text-center">
          <span class="text-base font-semibold text-ink-primary">API · SDK · GUI</span>
          <span class="text-xs text-ink-muted">TypeScript, Python, Go, or the dashboard — same engine, three access paths</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Use cases (new — production polish: social proof + concrete uses) -->
  <section class="relative border-t border-white/10">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-20">
      <p class="section-label">Use cases</p>
      <h2
        class="mt-6 max-w-3xl text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink-primary sm:text-3xl md:text-4xl"
        style="letter-spacing: -0.02em;"
      >
        Anywhere mobile Safari fidelity decides the outcome.
      </h2>
      <div class="mt-10 grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-3">
        <div class="card p-6">
          <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-glow-red">QA + testing</p>
          <h3 class="mt-3 text-lg font-semibold text-ink-primary">Mobile-Safari coverage</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-ink-secondary leading-relaxed">
            Run end-to-end tests against the exact engine your iOS
            users run. Same rendering, same JavaScript timing, same
            quirks — so the bug you reproduce in CI is the same bug
            your users hit in production.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card p-6">
          <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-glow-red">Data collection</p>
          <h3 class="mt-3 text-lg font-semibold text-ink-primary">Mobile-first scraping</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-ink-secondary leading-relaxed">
            Many sites serve different content to mobile Safari than to
            desktop Chrome. With Driftstack, your scraper sees what an
            iPhone visitor would see — no behind-the-scenes redirects to
            "please use our app" pages.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card p-6">
          <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-glow-red">Account workflows</p>
          <h3 class="mt-3 text-lg font-semibold text-ink-primary">Multi-account operations</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-ink-secondary leading-relaxed">
            Keep separate identities on services that lock down by device
            fingerprint. Persistent profiles let each account look like
            its own physical phone, every session, every time.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- R8 — Integrations strip. Outseta-style "works with your stack"
       row. Three SDK languages + four orchestration tools that
       customers commonly slot Driftstack into. Honest framing: only
       the 3 SDKs are first-party; the orchestration tools (Playwright,
       n8n, Make, Zapier) work because we expose a plain HTTPS API,
       not because we ship integration plugins. -->
  <section class="relative border-t border-white/10">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-16">
      <div class="grid gap-10 md:grid-cols-2 md:items-center">
        <div>
          <p class="section-label">Works with your stack</p>
          <h2
            class="mt-6 max-w-xl text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink-primary sm:text-3xl md:text-4xl"
            style="letter-spacing: -0.02em;"
          >
            Three SDKs. One HTTPS API. Slots into anything.
          </h2>
          <p class="mt-5 max-w-prose text-base text-ink-secondary leading-relaxed">
            First-party SDKs in TypeScript, Python, and Go. Beyond that, any
            HTTP-aware tool can drive Driftstack — Playwright tests, n8n
            workflows, Make.com scenarios, Zapier triggers, plain
            <code class="font-mono text-glow-red">curl</code> from a cron job.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-3 gap-3 text-center md:grid-cols-3">
          <div class="card p-4">
            <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.15em] text-glow-red">SDK</p>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm font-semibold text-ink-primary">TypeScript</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-ink-muted">@driftstack/sdk</p>
          </div>
          <div class="card p-4">
            <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.15em] text-glow-red">SDK</p>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm font-semibold text-ink-primary">Python</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-ink-muted">driftstack-sdk</p>
          </div>
          <div class="card p-4">
            <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.15em] text-glow-red">SDK</p>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm font-semibold text-ink-primary">Go</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-ink-muted">driftstack-go</p>
          </div>
          <div class="card p-4">
            <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.15em] text-ink-muted">via API</p>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm font-semibold text-ink-primary">Playwright</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-ink-muted">drives sessions</p>
          </div>
          <div class="card p-4">
            <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.15em] text-ink-muted">via API</p>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm font-semibold text-ink-primary">n8n · Make</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-ink-muted">workflow nodes</p>
          </div>
          <div class="card p-4">
            <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.15em] text-ink-muted">via API</p>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm font-semibold text-ink-primary">curl · cron</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-ink-muted">plain HTTPS</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- The detection problem (renamed from "Cumulative rig" — too jargony) -->
  <section class="relative border-t border-white/10">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-24">
      <p class="section-label">Why Driftstack works</p>
      <p
        class="mt-5 text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink-primary sm:mt-6 sm:text-5xl md:text-7xl lg:text-8xl"
        style="letter-spacing: -0.04em; line-height: 1.1;"
      >
        <span class="bg-gradient-to-br from-ink-primary via-ink-primary to-glow-red bg-clip-text text-transparent">
          One iPhone among millions.
        </span>
      </p>
      <p class="mt-6 max-w-prose text-sm text-ink-secondary leading-relaxed sm:mt-8 sm:text-base md:text-lg">
        Detection systems read hundreds of small signals — screen size,
        font rendering, GPU details, JavaScript timing — to decide what
        kind of device is visiting. Driftstack matches every signal
        against a reference iPhone, exactly. Not approximate, not "within
        tolerance". The canvas + WebGL hashes you return are the same
        hashes millions of real iPhones return — so when a fingerprinter
        groups visitors by hash, your session lands in the iPhone bucket
        with everyone else.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-prose text-base text-ink-secondary leading-relaxed">
        We match the iPhone families your users are actually on — iPhone
        15 Pro, iPhone 16 Pro, and the current iPhone 17 lineup, across
        the iOS versions Apple still ships Safari updates for (iOS 18.7
        / Safari 26.4, and Safari 26.5 as it rolls out). If any
        measurable signal differs from what the real phone sends, it's
        a launch-blocking bug — and we fix the engine, not a JavaScript
        wrapper around it.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-prose text-sm text-ink-muted">
        Full signal-by-signal methodology at
        <a href="/trust/cumulative-rig" class="font-mono text-glow-red underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-glow-red-soft">/trust/cumulative-rig</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- R8 — Fingerprint check visual. Replaces the previous text-only
       "we match every signal" claim with a side-by-side stub showing
       what a real iPhone vs. a typical stealth browser returns. The
       6-row table is a stand-in for the full /trust/cumulative-rig
       signal table; concrete numbers make the claim visually tangible
       instead of asking the prospect to trust prose. -->
  <section class="relative border-t border-white/10">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-20">
      <div class="grid gap-10 md:grid-cols-2 md:items-start">
        <div>
          <p class="section-label">What detection systems see</p>
          <h2
            class="mt-6 max-w-xl text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink-primary sm:text-3xl md:text-4xl"
            style="letter-spacing: -0.02em;"
          >
            Same signals as a physical iPhone. Not "close enough".
          </h2>
          <p class="mt-5 max-w-prose text-base text-ink-secondary leading-relaxed">
            Detection systems read hundreds of small signals. A typical
            stealth browser fakes the user-agent string but leaks the
            underlying engine in the canvas, WebGL, or audio fingerprint.
            Driftstack runs Apple's WebKit, so every signal returns the
            exact iPhone value — not approximate.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-ink-muted">
            A 7-signal preview. Full signal-by-signal table at
            <a
              href="/trust/cumulative-rig"
              class="font-mono text-glow-red underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-glow-red-soft"
              >/trust/cumulative-rig</a
            > — see
            <a
              href="/trust/security-overview"
              class="text-glow-red underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-glow-red-soft"
              >/trust/security-overview</a
            >
            for what's live today.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card overflow-hidden">
          <div class="grid grid-cols-[1fr_auto_auto] items-center gap-x-6 gap-y-3 px-6 py-5 text-sm">
            <span class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.15em] text-ink-muted">
              Signal
            </span>
            <span class="text-center font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.15em] text-ink-muted">
              Stealth Chromium
            </span>
            <span class="text-center font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.15em] text-glow-red">
              Driftstack
            </span>

            <span class="text-ink-primary">User-agent</span>
            <span class="text-center text-ink-muted">spoofed</span>
            <span class="text-center font-mono text-glow-red">real</span>

            <span class="text-ink-primary">Canvas hash</span>
            <span class="text-center text-ink-muted">leaks Chromium</span>
            <span class="text-center font-mono text-glow-red">iPhone</span>

            <span class="text-ink-primary">WebGL renderer</span>
            <span class="text-center text-ink-muted">leaks Chromium</span>
            <span class="text-center font-mono text-glow-red">Apple GPU</span>

            <span class="text-ink-primary">AudioContext</span>
            <span class="text-center text-ink-muted">leaks Chromium</span>
            <span class="text-center font-mono text-glow-red">iOS</span>

            <span class="text-ink-primary">Core Text metrics</span>
            <span class="text-center text-ink-muted">leaks system</span>
            <span class="text-center font-mono text-glow-red">iOS</span>

            <span class="text-ink-primary">JS engine timing</span>
            <span class="text-center text-ink-muted">V8 fingerprint</span>
            <span class="text-center font-mono text-glow-red">JSCore</span>

            <span class="text-ink-primary">Hash uniqueness across sessions</span>
            <span class="text-center text-ink-muted">100% unique</span>
            <span class="text-center font-mono text-glow-red">population-stable</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Stack — full-width statement -->
  <section class="relative border-t border-white/10">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-24">
      <p class="section-label">How it's built</p>
      <h2
        class="mt-6 max-w-4xl text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink-primary sm:text-3xl md:text-5xl"
        style="letter-spacing: -0.02em;"
      >
        Apple's engine. Not a Chromium copy.
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-6 max-w-prose text-lg text-ink-secondary leading-relaxed">
        Every stealth tool you've used before is a Chromium fork with a
        fake user-agent, or Playwright with a patch plugin layered on
        top. Driftstack runs Apple's WebKit source code itself — the
        same engine that ships on every iPhone. WebKit, Core Text, and
        the iOS rendering pipeline produce your fingerprint the way
        Apple wrote them, in the order Apple intended.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Metering — single dense paragraph + data point -->
  <section class="relative border-t border-white/10">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-24">
      <div class="grid gap-12 md:grid-cols-[1fr_auto] md:items-end">
        <div>
          <p class="section-label">Metering</p>
          <h2
            class="mt-6 max-w-3xl text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink-primary sm:text-3xl md:text-4xl"
            style="letter-spacing: -0.02em;"
          >
            One metric. Concurrent sessions. That's it.
          </h2>
          <p class="mt-6 max-w-prose text-base text-ink-secondary leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-ink-primary">Concurrent</strong> means
            sessions running at the same time — think browser tabs. Pick a
            plan, get a concurrent cap, run anything inside it. No
            per-call markup. No per-element fees. No hourly metering that
            turns idle sessions into surprise overage charges. Visit
            200 pages on one session for the cost of visiting one.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="accent-rule flex flex-col gap-1">
          <span class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-glow-red">
            Concurrent cap
          </span>
          <span
            class="font-mono text-5xl font-semibold text-ink-primary"
            style="letter-spacing: 0.02em;"
          >
